<?php
/** en strings of the inner pages (generated from copy-inner.json plus hand-written labels). */
return array(
	'ab_intro' => 'Madoka Childcare is a centre for babies and toddlers of working families, in a brick house on Linden Row in Ranelagh. It has been open since 2015, with 39 places for children from 4 months to 4 years.',
	'ab_h1' => 'A small house for small people',
	'ab_b1' => 'Madoka Quinlan opened Madoka Childcare in 2015 after twelve years as a room leader. She wanted working parents to have a house where the same adults see a child from the door at 07:30 until pickup. The centre is a two-storey brick house with a back garden at 12 Linden Row, Ranelagh, Dublin 6. We take children from 4 months to 4 years, and we have 39 places in three rooms. Hours are Monday to Friday, 07:30 to 18:30. We close at weekends, on Irish public holidays, and from 24 December to 1 January. The day itself is simple to name: hello at the door, breakfast, time outside, lunch, rest, studio play, and a story before home. We describe our staffing as our own policy. We do not present it as a ranking, and we do not speak about later school results.',
	'ab_h2' => 'Why three rooms',
	'ab_b2' => 'Three rooms keep the day matched to the child. Nest is 4 to 15 months, 9 places, with one educator for every three babies. Meadow is 15 to 30 months, 12 places, one educator to four children. Grove is two and a half to four years, 18 places, one educator to six children. Those ratios are the numbers we staff to, and nine plus twelve plus eighteen is the whole house. A small group is how an adult hears a new word and notices a tired face before the afternoon frays. Children move between rooms when age and ease line up, after you and the key person have talked it through. A birthday on its own does not move a child. The kitchen and the back garden are shared. The pace inside each room stays its own.',
	'ab_h3' => 'A key person, every day',
	'ab_b3' => 'Every child has a key person. That educator greets your child, watches the small changes, and is the adult you speak to about food, sleep and settling in. Samuel Osei leads the Nest, with Priya Raman beside him. Aoife Brennan leads the Meadow and is deputy director. Clara Moreau leads the Grove, and Jonas Weber leads garden time with the older children. Madoka Quinlan is founder and centre director. When a key person is away, another educator in the same room, who already knows your child, takes the day and tells you so at the door. Our hiring policy is that every educator holds a QQI Level 6 early-childhood award or higher. That rule describes who we employ. It is not a claim about your child\'s future.',
	'ab_h4' => 'Outside every day, and a slow start',
	'ab_b4' => 'Outdoor time is a part of the day. At 09:30, children who are ready go into the back garden to dig, walk and use the low planters and the willow tunnel. Wet weather changes the clothes. Babies in the Nest go out in shorter stretches, close to their own feeds and sleep. Settling in is slow on purpose. You visit on a Tuesday or a Thursday, at 10:00 or at 16:00. If you accept a place, you pay the €150 registration once, meet the key person to talk through routines, food and sleep, and then use a fortnight of shorter days before the first full day. That fortnight is included in the first month\'s fee. We would rather a child know the room before a parent is gone for a full day.',
	'cd_intro' => 'The care day runs from 07:30 to 18:30, Monday to Friday, with the same nine landmarks in every room. Babies in the Nest still feed and sleep when they need to.',
	'cd_h1' => 'Hour by hour',
	'cd_b1' => 'Doors open at 07:30 and close at 18:30, Monday to Friday. The house uses one shared shape so you can picture the day: hello and breakfast, a morning circle, the garden, lunch, rest, a snack, studio play, stories, and the last goodbyes. The clock tells you where your child is likely to be. A child who arrives at 08:10 still has breakfast. A child who is deep in water play is given time to finish. You should be able to ask what happens after lunch and hear a plain answer. Babies in the Nest follow this shape only as far as their bodies allow. Their feeds and naps stay on demand, which the last section of this page explains.',
	'cd_h2' => 'From the door to the garden',
	'cd_b2' => 'At 07:30 the doors open, there is a hello at the door, and breakfast is ready for children who need it. Coats go on low hooks, and any note from home is taken before you leave. At 09:00 the rooms gather for morning circle: a song, who is here today, and a look at what the garden or the studio will hold. It is a few minutes of gathering, and then the children move. At 09:30 garden and outdoor play begin. Sand, small spades, low planters and the willow tunnel fill the rest of the morning, with educators in the play. Nest babies join for as long as they are comfortable, then come back to the mat, the milk and the quiet.',
	'cd_h3' => 'Lunch and rest',
	'cd_b3' => 'At 11:30 the house sits down for lunch. Tomás Rivera has cooked it that morning, and the rooms eat at low tables with small spoons. Educators sit with the children. Allergies and other dietary needs are already in the kitchen notes, so the plate matches what you agreed. At 12:30 the rooms turn to nap and quiet rest. Children who sleep use the quiet space. Children who have finished with a daytime sleep rest with a book or a basket nearby. The room stays dim and plain until people wake and the day turns toward the snack. A baby in the Nest who is asleep is left to sleep. A baby who is awake is not put down because the older rooms are resting.',
	'cd_h4' => 'Afternoon until the doors close',
	'cd_b4' => 'At 15:00 the afternoon snack is set out, from the same kitchen as lunch. At 15:30 studio play begins: paint, blocks, water and music, with aprons and a floor that can be washed. Children may stay with one material. At 17:00 the rooms come back for stories and first pickups. The key person, or the educator covering that day, has one true thing to tell you about the hours just gone. At 18:30 is the last pickup, and the doors close. If your plan changes, call +353 1 555 0172 so your child hears a clear update from someone they know. Weekends, Irish public holidays, and 24 December to 1 January are already closed days, so there is no late door on those dates.',
	'cd_h5' => 'Babies keep their own rhythm',
	'cd_b5' => 'The nine times are the spine of the house. They are not a script for a young baby. In the Nest, naps and feeds stay on demand from 4 months through to about 15 months. Milk at 10:00 happens at 10:00, even when the toddlers are in the garden. Sleep that comes before 12:30 is real sleep, and sleep that comes later is still allowed. Samuel Osei and Priya Raman note the day\'s feeds and sleeps so you can hear them at pickup. Floor play, a short time outside, and a familiar adult still happen, fitted around the baby. As a child moves toward Meadow, the shared parts of the day grow longer, and you hear about that change before it starts.',
	'cd_h6' => 'The whole day',
	'cd_b6' => 'Read straight down, the day is fixed. 07:30, doors open, hello at the door, breakfast. 09:00, morning circle. 09:30, garden and outdoor play. 11:30, lunch. 12:30, nap and quiet rest. 15:00, afternoon snack. 15:30, studio play: paint, blocks, water, music. 17:00, stories and first pickups. 18:30, last pickup, doors close. We are closed at weekends, on Irish public holidays, and from 24 December to 1 January. If you want the feel of one hour rather than the list, visit on Tuesday or Thursday at 10:00 or 16:00. A visit lasts 45 minutes, in the rooms, while the day is actually happening. Hanna Kowalski keeps the visit book.',
	'rm_intro' => 'Three rooms share one house at 12 Linden Row. Together they offer 39 places for children from 4 months to 4 years, grouped so the day fits the age.',
	'rm_h1' => 'Three rooms, grouped by age',
	'rm_b1' => 'The house holds 39 places for children from 4 months to 4 years, divided into Nest, Meadow and Grove. Each room has a lead educator and its own key people, so the furniture, the noise and the rest match the children inside it. A move happens when age and the child\'s ease line up, and you hear the plan before the first morning in the new room. The ratios on this page are the numbers we staff to. They are our policy for these rooms. Outdoor play at 09:30 and the kitchen belong to everyone. The Nest can stay quiet while the Grove is at the water table. Two-day places begin at 15 months, in Meadow and Grove, and are not offered for babies in the Nest.',
	'rm_h2' => 'Nest',
	'rm_b2' => 'Nest is for babies from 4 months to 15 months. There are 9 places, and our ratio is one educator to three babies. Samuel Osei has been Nest room lead since 2017. Priya Raman has been a Nest educator since 2019. The room is built low: a soft mat, a mirror rail at crawling height, and a nap space where a baby sleeps when sleep comes. Feeds are on demand. At the family meeting you tell us about milk, the way you feed at home, and any allergy, and we follow that account. The day still includes a hello at 07:30, time on the floor, a short spell outside, and a known person at pickup. Settling in is the same fortnight the whole house uses, often while a parent is returning to work, and the first days stay short.',
	'rm_h3' => 'Meadow',
	'rm_b3' => 'Meadow is for children from 15 months to 30 months. There are 12 places, and our ratio is one educator to four children. Aoife Brennan, deputy director, has led the room since 2016. This is an age of early sentences, strong feelings, and sleep that is changing. Some children still nap at 12:30. Others rest beside a book. Lunch is at the low table. A child on the way from purées to baby-led weaning gets the texture you and the key person have agreed. The garden is daily work here: sand, digging, an adult on the ground. Two-day, three-day, four-day and five-day patterns are all possible in this room. Twins, and any two children in the same family, each keep their own key person so the morning at the door stays unhurried.',
	'rm_h4' => 'Grove',
	'rm_b4' => 'Grove is for children from two and a half to four years. There are 18 places, and our ratio is one educator to six children. Clara Moreau has led the room since 2018. Jonas Weber has been a Grove educator since 2020, with garden and outdoor play as his particular work. Studio play at 15:30 — paint, blocks, water, music — runs longest here, and washing hands is part of that work. Stories at 17:00 are often when a parent on a late shift arrives. Older children still need a lap and a known adult. A child who began in the Nest may be with us through to Grove, and the leaving is talked through with the key person while the ordinary day continues. The back garden, lunch at 11:30, and the same door at 07:30 stay in place until the last week.',
	'ed_intro' => 'Eight people run Madoka Childcare. Your child is meant to know the educators in their own room, and you are meant to know who leads that room and who arranges your visit.',
	'ed_h1' => 'Your key person',
	'ed_b1' => 'The key person is the educator who holds your child in mind. They greet at the door when they are in, notice the small changes, and meet you before the first full day to talk about routines, food and sleep. Other adults in the room know your child too, because we staff to our ratios: one to three in the Nest, one to four in the Meadow, one to six in the Grove. The key person is the one responsible for the link with you. If they are out, a named colleague in the same room takes the day and says so at pickup. Every educator we hire holds a QQI Level 6 early-childhood award or higher. That is our hiring policy, and it stops there. At the family meeting we also write down the languages you use at home, so we can follow words your child already uses.',
	'ed_h2' => 'Direction and room leads',
	'ed_b2' => 'Madoka Quinlan is founder and centre director, here since 2015. She opened the house after twelve years as a room leader, and she still knows the rooms from the floor, not only from the office. Aoife Brennan joined in 2016 as deputy director and Meadow room lead. She keeps the day steady for children from 15 to 30 months, and she is the person on the floor when Madoka is away from it. Samuel Osei has been Nest room lead since 2017. He follows feeds, sleep and the slow first weeks with babies from 4 to 15 months. Clara Moreau has been Grove room lead since 2018. She holds the older room, from two and a half to four years, including studio play at 15:30 and stories at 17:00, when the first parents arrive.',
	'ed_h3' => 'In the rooms, the kitchen and enrolment',
	'ed_b3' => 'Priya Raman has been a Nest educator since 2019. She works with Samuel on the mat and at the door, and she is often the key person for a baby in their first year here. Jonas Weber joined in 2020 as a Grove educator, responsible for garden and outdoor play. At 09:30 he is one of the adults in the sand with the older children. Tomás Rivera has been the cook since 2016. He is in the kitchen through the morning so that lunch at 11:30 and the afternoon snack at 15:00 are cooked in the house. Hanna Kowalski has been family coordinator since 2021. She looks after enrolment and visits, which run on Tuesday and Thursday at 10:00 and at 16:00. For a visit, or a question about a place, write to hello@madoka-childcare.example or call +353 1 555 0172.',
	'fd_intro' => 'Lunch and the afternoon snack are cooked in the kitchen at 12 Linden Row by Tomás Rivera, who has been here since 2016. Meals are included in the monthly fee.',
	'fd_h1' => 'Cooked here, every morning',
	'fd_b1' => 'Tomás Rivera has been the cook since 2016. He is in the kitchen while the door opens at 07:30, and lunch is ready for 11:30. The work happens in the house: vegetables on the board, pots on the stove, bright tiles, and no delivery left at the kerb in place of cooking. Educators sit and eat with the children at the low tables. The meal follows the children who are actually here, including a Nest baby on purées and a toddler feeding themselves. Breakfast is out for early arrivals who are hungry. The afternoon snack follows rest, at 15:00. We cook carefully and we listen when you tell us what your child cannot eat. We do not claim that a menu will change a child\'s health.',
	'fd_h2' => 'This week\'s lunches',
	'fd_b2' => 'Each weekday has a cooked lunch at 11:30 and an afternoon snack at 15:00. Monday\'s lunch is cooked on Monday morning, and Monday\'s snack is set out after rest. Tuesday, Wednesday, Thursday and Friday follow that same shape, with the dishes changing as the week is planned. Tomás writes the coming lunches and snacks where you can read them at drop-off, and he adjusts the list for the season and for who is in the rooms. The pattern stays plain: food a child can eat with a small spoon or with their hands, then a simple snack, with water on the table. Breakfast sits alongside this from 07:30. It is for children who arrive needing it. If you want the current week before a visit, ask Hanna Kowalski and she will have it ready.',
	'fd_h3' => 'Allergies, purées and weaning',
	'fd_b3' => 'Tell us about allergies and other dietary needs at the family meeting, before the first full day, and tell Tomás again if something changes. The kitchen keeps that note where the food is plated. We follow it. If a meal cannot be made safe for your child, we say so before you rely on the place. Babies in the Nest have purées. Children who are ready move toward baby-led weaning, with soft pieces from the same lunch, and you and the key person set the pace. Preferences belong in the note as well as allergies, so a plate is not a surprise at 11:30. Meals are included in the monthly fee, together with nappies, wipes and sun cream. There is no separate food bill at the end of the month.',
	'fe_intro' => 'Fees are monthly, in euro, and the six amounts on this page are the amounts we charge. Meals, nappies, wipes and sun cream are included, and the year is billed as 12 payments.',
	'fe_h1' => 'The monthly fees',
	'fe_b1' => 'Fees are in euro and are paid in 12 monthly payments. Five days, 07:30 to 18:30, Monday to Friday, is €1,480 a month. Four days, any four fixed weekdays, is €1,240 a month. Three days, any three fixed weekdays, is €980 a month. Two days, from 15 months in Meadow and Grove, is €690 a month. An extra day, booked one week ahead if a place is free, is €78. Registration, paid once when you accept a place, is €150. The settling-in fortnight is included in the first month\'s fee. The weekdays you book stay fixed, so the room can staff to them. Two-day places are for Meadow and Grove, from 15 months, and are not offered in the Nest. Notice, when you hold a place and decide to leave, is one month.',
	'fe_h2' => 'What\'s included',
	'fe_b2' => 'The monthly figure covers the day you have booked. Inside it are meals cooked here — breakfast for early arrivals, lunch at 11:30, and the afternoon snack at 15:00 — plus nappies, wipes and sun cream. You do not send a separate payment to the kitchen, and you do not bring the day\'s nappies in a bag. The registration fee of €150 sits outside the monthly line and is paid once, when you accept a place. A visit on Tuesday or Thursday, at 10:00 or 16:00, is not charged. The extra day at €78 is only for a day you add, a week ahead, when that room still has a free place. If it does not, we say so. We do not add a child beyond the 9, 12 or 18 places in Nest, Meadow and Grove.',
	'fe_h3' => 'Hours, closures and payment',
	'fe_b3' => 'We are open Monday to Friday, 07:30 to 18:30. Visits are on Tuesday and Thursday at 10:00 and at 16:00. We are closed at weekends, on Irish public holidays, and from 24 December to 1 January. Payment is by direct debit, in 12 monthly payments across the year. The published monthly amount is the amount in a month that contains a public holiday or the Christmas closure. The fee table carries no holiday surcharge and no seasonal reduction. Your child\'s place continues through those closed days. One month\'s notice is how a place is given up. If you need to change the number of days, speak to Hanna Kowalski before the next debit so the room and the payment match. Call +353 1 555 0172 or write to hello@madoka-childcare.example.',
	'fe_h4' => 'Statements for the National Childcare Scheme',
	'fe_b4' => 'If you apply to the National Childcare Scheme, you may need paperwork from us. We provide the statements families need for that scheme. The amount you receive depends on your family. We do not estimate it at the door, and we do not fold a guessed subsidy into the fee. The prices on this page stay €1,480, €1,240, €980, €690, €78 and €150, whether or not a subsidy is in place. Hanna Kowalski, family coordinator since 2021, prepares the statements and can tell you which dates and which child they cover. She cannot tell you what the scheme will decide. If you are still choosing the centre, start with a visit. Ask for the statements when an application actually needs them.',
	'en_intro' => 'Enrolment has five steps, from a 45-minute visit to the first full day. The settling-in fortnight is included in the first month\'s fee, and registration is €150 when you accept a place.',
	'en_h1' => 'The waiting list',
	'en_b1' => 'If the room that fits your child\'s age is full, Hanna Kowalski keeps a waiting list. Nest has 9 places for 4 to 15 months, Meadow has 12 for 15 to 30 months, and Grove has 18 for two and a half to four years. A place is offered when one of those 39 places is free on the days you need. There is no fee to wait. We look at when you asked, and at whether the weekdays you want match a real gap. Two-day patterns start from 15 months. When an offer is made, the €150 registration is paid only if you accept. Until you accept, the place is not held. Hanna writes the date by which she needs your answer, and after that date she offers the place to the next family whose days fit.',
	'en_h2' => 'Visit, offer, family meeting',
	'en_b2' => 'Step 01 is a visit on Tuesday or Thursday, at 10:00 or at 16:00, for 45 minutes. You see the room, the garden, and the door your child would use. Step 02 is a place offer and the €150 registration, paid once when you accept. Step 03 is a family meeting with your key person. You talk through routines, food and sleep: what a morning looks like at home, what your child eats, how they settle to sleep, and the languages you speak together. The key person writes this in plain notes. The meeting is a handover of real information, so the first day is not a guess. Samuel Osei, Aoife Brennan, Clara Moreau, or the educator who will be your key person, is the one who sits down with you.',
	'en_h3' => 'The fortnight, then the first full day',
	'en_b3' => 'Step 04 is the settling-in fortnight. Days start short and grow toward a full day, 07:30 to 18:30, at the pace your child can use. You may stay in the room, then leave for a short while, then leave for a longer morning. The key person tells you what they saw, in ordinary language. The fortnight is included in the first month\'s fee. Step 05 is the first full day. By then your child has already eaten here, rested here, and seen you come back. If the fortnight shows that a full day is too soon, we keep the short days going rather than skip ahead. Once you hold the place, notice is one month. If during settling in you decide the house is the wrong fit, tell Hanna early and she will apply that one-month notice to your start.',
	'en_h4' => 'What to bring',
	'en_b4' => 'Bring a spare set of clothes and, if your child wants it, one comfort object from home. Shoes for the garden can stay by the door. Nappies, wipes and sun cream for the day here are included, and so are lunch and the afternoon snack, so you do not need to pack those. Milk feeds for a Nest baby follow what you agreed at the family meeting. Talk to Samuel Osei or Priya Raman about what you will carry in. Put your child\'s name on clothes that must go home. Leave treasured toys at home if breaking them would matter. If your child needs medicine on a given day, speak to the key person first and give written instructions you and your prescriber stand over. We follow those instructions, and we say so if the room cannot carry them out safely.',
	'st_intro' => 'Three families are written about here: the Haddads, the Byrne-Okafors and the Lindqvists. The accounts are fictional sample content, shared in the spirit of permission and edited lightly.',
	'st_h1' => 'The Haddad family',
	'st_b1' => 'Lina started in the Nest at 7 months. Her mother was returning to work, so a new room and a longer day arrived together. Settling in took the fortnight we use for every child. On the first mornings her mother stayed low on the mat while Lina crawled toward Samuel Osei and the mirror rail. Then her mother left for a short time and came back before lunch. By the second week Lina could take a feed with Priya Raman, sleep when she was tired, and look up when the door opened. The garden became the part her mother asked about first. Even as a baby Lina had a short time outside at 09:30, and later there was sand on her hands. Her mother said the garden was where she could picture the morning she was missing, and she let us keep that sentence. Those two weeks stayed with Lina\'s own feeds, her own key person, and a clock held lightly.',
	'st_h2' => 'The Byrne-Okafor family',
	'st_b2' => 'The Byrne-Okafor twins are in the Meadow. Two children of the same age can make a doorway crowded at 07:30, so the family asked for a calm handover. We used the rule the house already keeps: one key person for each twin, rather than one educator trying to tell both stories in a single minute. Each twin is greeted by their own person. Coats go on the low hooks. There is a word with whichever parent has the buggy that morning. Aoife Brennan leads the room and keeps the two key people aligned, so the twins are known as two children. At 09:30 they are often both in the sand, sometimes side by side and sometimes not. The family told us the gain was practical. Mornings stopped feeling like a queue. Pickup brought two notes, including whether either child had slept at 12:30. We staffed the door the way we staff every child\'s morning.',
	'st_h3' => 'The Lindqvist family',
	'st_b3' => 'Elias moved from the Nest to the Grove over three years. He began as a baby in Samuel Osei\'s room and, three years on, was with Clara Moreau\'s older children, and with Jonas Weber in the garden at 09:30. The time between was a run of ordinary days: lunch at 11:30, rest at 12:30, paint and water at 15:30, stories at 17:00. What his parents needed us to understand was the end of the day. One of them works hospital shifts, so pickup is often late, toward 17:00 and sometimes close to 18:30. Stories at 17:00 are already the first pickups, and a child whose adult is still at work stays in that story, then in quiet play, with someone he knows. His parents said a shift day still looked like the same room. We close at 18:30 even then. They call if the time moves, and the room says who will be there.',
	'st_h4' => 'How these accounts were written',
	'st_b4' => 'The Haddad, Byrne-Okafor and Lindqvist stories are shared in the form a family allows: we draft from the way the rooms work, and we edit lightly. They are fictional sample content, written so you can hear particular days — a baby at 7 months, a fortnight back at work, two key people for twins, three years from Nest to Grove, late pickups on hospital-shift days. They are not ratings, and they are not a result you can expect for your own child. Lina, the twins, and Elias stand in for situations we do actually meet. The care day, the rooms and the fees remain the binding detail. To see the house, book a visit on Tuesday or Thursday at 10:00 or 16:00, for 45 minutes, through Hanna Kowalski on +353 1 555 0172 or hello@madoka-childcare.example.',
	'fq_intro' => 'These are the questions parents ask before they book a visit. The answers match the fees, the hours and the settling-in fortnight used everywhere else on the site.',
	'fq_h1' => 'What do you do if my child is sick?',
	'fq_b1' => 'Call +353 1 555 0172 in the morning if your child will not be in, even if you are still unsure. If they become unwell during the day and cannot manage the ordinary room, we call you to collect them. We want a sick child at home, with you, rather than propped up in the group so a meeting can finish. We do not publish a temperature cut-off or a set number of hours you must wait before returning. Come back when your child is well enough for a room day, and speak to the key person if you are unsure. A day at home does not create a refund and it does not add a fee. The monthly payment holds the place, at the amount you already pay, whether that is €1,480, €1,240, €980 or €690.',
	'fq_h2' => 'Do I need to bring nappies?',
	'fq_b2' => 'No. Nappies, wipes and sun cream are included in the monthly fee, together with meals. You do not need a daily supply for the hours between 07:30 and 18:30. If your child needs a particular type because of a reaction, say so at the family meeting and we will use what we have agreed. If you prefer that we use a cream you supply, hand it to the educator with your child\'s name on it. We keep it for your child alone. Spare clothes still come from home, because a full change of clothing is not part of the fee. Lunch at 11:30 and the snack at 15:00 are included as well, so the bag at the door can stay light: clothes, a comfort object if you want one, and anything we have asked you to carry for a Nest milk feed.',
	'fq_h3' => 'How do naps work?',
	'fq_b3' => 'At 12:30 the house has nap and quiet rest. Children who sleep can sleep. Children who have grown out of a nap rest with a book or a basket, without being asked to lie still for a stretch chosen for the timetable. In the Nest, naps and feeds stay on demand all day. A baby is not woken to join the garden, and is not settled to sleep only because Meadow is on the mats. At pickup the key person can tell you when your child slept and how they seemed afterwards. That is news from the day, not a chart to improve. If sleep is changing at home, say so, and we will change the mat and the timing with you. The note titled "Why naps don\'t follow the clock" says more about this, in the same practical spirit.',
	'fq_h4' => 'What is your policy on phones and photographs?',
	'fq_b4' => 'Educators do not publish photographs of your child. Observation notes stay in plain notebooks in the room, for the key person and for you. We ask parents and visitors to keep phones away during their time in the rooms, and not to photograph other people\'s children. A picture of your own child at pickup, taken with the educator aware, is yours. A picture that includes someone else\'s child is not for sharing. Visit mornings and afternoons follow the same request. We do not run a public photo feed of the rooms, and we do not invite media in. If a tradesperson must cross the room, Hanna tells the families affected. The garden, the studio and the nap space are the children\'s day. They are not a backdrop, and the children are not material for a post.',
	'fq_h5' => 'What happens if I am late for pickup?',
	'fq_b5' => 'First pickups begin at 17:00, around stories. The last pickup is 18:30, and the doors close. If your plan changes, call +353 1 555 0172 so the educator can tell your child something true. Until you arrive, and as long as that is still before the close, your child stays with a known adult in the room. The fee list has no late-pickup charge. The boundary we do set is 18:30. Some families, including parents on hospital shifts, collect toward the end of stories or later in the last hour, and the room already works that way. The close still stands. If late arrivals become the usual pattern, Hanna Kowalski will talk with you about whether the days you have booked still fit the work you do. A call on the day is always better than a child watching the door with no news.',
	'fq_h6' => 'When are you closed for holidays?',
	'fq_b6' => 'We open Monday to Friday only. Weekends are closed. Irish public holidays are closed. We are also closed from 24 December to 1 January. Visits run on Tuesday and Thursday at 10:00 and 16:00 only when the house is open, so none are held in that Christmas closure or on a public holiday. Monthly fees stay the published amounts, paid in 12 payments by direct debit. The table adds no closure surcharge and shows no holiday rebate. The place continues through the days we are shut. You do not have to book an extra day to stand in for a closure. If you want an additional day another week, and a place is free, that is €78, booked one week ahead. Otherwise the garden, the kitchen and the rooms are simply closed.',
	'fq_h7' => 'How do you handle allergies?',
	'fq_b7' => 'Tell the key person and Tomás Rivera, in writing, at the family meeting or as soon as you know. Say what your child must avoid and what you do at home. We will not invent a medical plan, and we will not guess at a dose. Lunch at 11:30 and the snack at 15:00 are plated from that note. If a meal cannot be kept away from a food your child must avoid, we tell you before you depend on us. Purées and baby-led pieces follow the same rule. A strong dislike can go in the note as well, so the plate is familiar. We will not describe the house as free of any allergen. A kitchen that cooks for many children cannot make that kind of claim. What we do is read the note at every service, and cook the week\'s lunches with those children in mind.',
	'fq_h8' => 'How long does settling in last?',
	'fq_b8' => 'Settling in is a fortnight. Days start short and grow toward full days of 07:30 to 18:30. It is step 04, after the visit, the place offer with the €150 registration, and the family meeting with your key person. The fortnight is included in the first month\'s fee. There is no shorter paid option and no longer one at another price. If a child, especially a baby in the Nest, needs the short days to continue, we extend that shape. The first full day is step 05, after your child has seen you leave and come back. Parents often return to work during the same two weeks. Bring that date to the family meeting. We will plan the hours with it in view, and we will say clearly if a full day on that date is too soon. The child\'s pace is the one we follow.',
	'fq_h9' => 'Do you offer a sibling rate?',
	'fq_b9' => 'Siblings are welcome when each child has a place in the right room. A baby in the Nest and a child in the Grove are two places, two key people and two fees. The fee table has no sibling discount. Five days are €1,480 a month for each child, four days €1,240, three days €980, and two days €690 from 15 months in Meadow or Grove. Registration is €150 for each place you accept. Twins are enrolled the same way. As with the Byrne-Okafor family, we aim for one key person each, so 07:30 stays calm and each child has their own note at pickup. We cannot reserve a future baby\'s place years ahead. When you know you will need a room, ask Hanna Kowalski to add the sibling to the waiting list, and she will say whether the days you already use can line up.',
	'vi_intro' => 'Visits run on Tuesday and Thursday at 10:00 and 16:00, for 45 minutes, while the rooms are open. The house is at 12 Linden Row, Ranelagh, Dublin 6.',
	'vi_h1' => 'Visits on Tuesday and Thursday',
	'vi_b1' => 'Come on Tuesday or Thursday, at 10:00 or at 16:00. The visit lasts 45 minutes. You see the room that fits your child\'s age, the back garden, and the way educators sit with children at a low table. Hanna Kowalski arranges the visits. She will ask your child\'s age so the time is spent in the Nest, the Meadow or the Grove, among the children who would be their group. This is a look at the house while it is working. It is not a scored interview. If you later accept a place, you pay the €150 registration once, meet the key person, take the settling-in fortnight, and then come to the first full day. To ask for a time, write to hello@madoka-childcare.example or call +353 1 555 0172. We are open Monday to Friday, 07:30 to 18:30.',
	'vi_h2' => 'Find us',
	'vi_b2' => 'Madoka Childcare is at 12 Linden Row, Ranelagh, Dublin 6, Ireland, in a two-storey brick house with a back garden. The Ranelagh Luas stop is 4 minutes on foot. Bike parking with child seats is there for 12 bikes, and a buggy shed takes prams you would rather not fold into the hall. The door you use on a visit is the same door children use at 07:30, with low hooks for coats just inside. Please arrive at the time you booked, so the educator meeting you can step out of the room without leaving a gap. We are closed at weekends, on Irish public holidays, and from 24 December to 1 January, and a visit is not held on a closed day. If you are coming by bike, the 12 child-seat spaces are beside the house.',
	'vi_h3' => 'What the enquiry asks',
	'vi_b3' => 'The enquiry form asks for your name, your child\'s name and age, the days you hope for, and whether Tuesday or Thursday, at 10:00 or at 16:00, suits you. It asks for a phone number and an email so Hanna can reply. You do not need to attach documents. If the age is under 4 months or over 4 years, she will say so, because those are the ages we take, across 39 places. Sending the form does not reserve a place. A place exists when we offer one and you accept it, with the €150 registration. For anything that concerns a child who is with us today, call +353 1 555 0172 rather than waiting on the form. Madoka Quinlan is the director. Hanna Kowalski, family coordinator since 2021, is the person who answers visit requests.',
	'nt_intro' => 'Notes from the rooms are short written pieces about food, sleep, hands, paint, rain and the first morning. They sit beside the care day, and they do not replace the fees or the hours.',
	'nt_h1' => 'From the rooms',
	'nt_b1' => 'Notes from the rooms are short pieces about ordinary parts of the day: the kitchen, sleep, hand-washing, paint, rain in the garden, and a first morning at the door. The people who write them are the people who work here, including Tomás Rivera in the kitchen and the educators who keep the Nest rest honest. A note shows the thinking behind something you might only hear about at pickup. The pieces are not ratings, awards or reports in the press. The centre has been here since 2015, and the notes stay with the practice we run: the ratios we staff to, meals inside the fee, and naps that follow a baby. If a note and the fees page ever seemed to disagree, the fees and the care day are the record we keep.',
	'nt_h2' => 'The six notes',
	'nt_b2' => 'Six notes are published. "What we cook in a week" follows Tomás\'s morning in the kitchen, lunch at 11:30 and the snack at 15:00. "Why naps don\'t follow the clock" is about rest at 12:30, and about Nest babies who sleep on demand. "Washing hands, forty times a day" is about the low sink, soap, and the pause before food and after paint. "Paint, water and mess" is about studio play at 15:30. "Rainy-day garden" is about going outside at 09:30 when the back garden is wet. "The first morning" is about a 07:30 arrival during settling in, a coat on a low hook, and a parent who stays for one minute more. The three newest are the cookery note, the nap note and the hand-washing note. Dates on a note do not change the hours or the monthly fees.',
	'nt_h3' => 'Reading a note before you visit',
	'nt_b3' => 'A note will not decide enrolment for you. It will show how we talk about mess, weather, food and goodbye. If "The first morning" makes you want to see that door, book Tuesday or Thursday at 10:00 or 16:00. The visit is 45 minutes. If the kitchen note raises a question about an allergy, bring it to the family meeting with your key person and with Tomás Rivera, rather than leaving it as a general worry. The house is at 12 Linden Row, Ranelagh, Dublin 6. Madoka Quinlan is founder and centre director. Hanna Kowalski arranges visits. The phone is +353 1 555 0172 and the email is hello@madoka-childcare.example. Children are not named in the notes. The Haddad, Byrne-Okafor and Lindqvist families are written on their own page.',
